cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Decisive Shock or Strategic Exhaustion? A Dynamical Framework for War Termination Mechanisms"
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: Enholm
    given-names: Jake
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  We develop a computational framework for distinguishing between two competing
  hypotheses of war termination: the Mahan hypothesis (decisive battles) and the
  attrition hypothesis (cumulative exhaustion). Using a dynamical simulation
  model applied to 4,220+ wars, we show that decisive events and attritional
  processes are not competing explanations but interacting mechanisms occurring
  at different phases of conflict. The paper demonstrates that historical
  classification of wars as "decisive" or "attritional" often reflects hindsight
  bias, and that structural indicators contain partial but not complete predictive
  information about termination mechanisms.
keywords:
  - war termination
  - decisive battle
  - strategic exhaustion
  - computational history
  - dynamical simulation
  - Mahan
  - Clausewitz
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/jenholm/mahan-vs-attrition"
date-released: "2026-07-17"
